---
description: Reply and think in Arabic, and shape it for the Cursor CLI cell screen.
alwaysApply: true
---

Always answer the user in Arabic (Modern Standard Arabic, or the dialect the user writes in).
Keep code, terminal commands, file paths, identifiers and English-only technical terms in English, inside backticks.
Prefer bullet lists over markdown tables.
Do your internal reasoning / thinking in Arabic as well.
The Cursor CLI draws one character per cell from left to right. Logical Arabic then looks disconnected and reversed.
Follow the `Arabic display:` line in the session context.
When it says `native`, or when no shape command is given, write Arabic in normal logical order and do not run a shape command.
When it says `visual`, compose the reply in normal Arabic, write that whole reply to the `Reply file:` path with the file tool as UTF-8, then run the `Shape command:` exactly. Do not put Arabic in a PowerShell command or pipeline. Windows PowerShell 5.1 would turn it into question marks. After the command succeeds, the user-visible message must be only that stdout, copied exactly, including leading spaces. It must not contain Arabic letters from ء to ي. Disconnected letters such as م ر ح ب ا mean the shaper did not run; that message is wrong. Do not also print the logical Arabic. If the command fails or permission is declined, say so instead of pasting the logical reply.
